# ==========================================================================
#  O65  Richness and coupling: what the metrics count, and what a style choice does to them
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    The richness metrics are read with their small print: they count
#    properties by declared domain, so an ontology that keeps domains
#    sparse on principle scores low on richness, and gist does.
#
#  WHY
#    AROnto is attributes per class, INROnto relationships per class,
#    RROnto the share of properties that are attributes, ANOnto annotations
#    per class, CROnto individuals per class. The paper does not say how a
#    property is assigned to a class, and the only workable reading is by
#    rdfs:domain: a property belongs to the class it is declared on.
#    scripts/metrics.py does that, and counts each member of a union
#    domain.
#
#    The consequence is on the diagram. The Trail declares a domain on
#    nearly every property, so it scores 5 on attribute and relationship
#    richness. gist declares domains sparingly, as a matter of principle
#    set out in its README and in o31, so it scores 1 and 2 on the same
#    metrics with sixty-six object properties and fifty datatype properties
#    in the file. Nothing is poorer about gist; a design choice the metric
#    cannot see has been counted as an absence. That is the general lesson
#    about richness metrics, and it applies to CROnto too, which scores
#    every pure vocabulary 1 because a schema has no individuals.
#
#    ANOnto is the one to watch, because it measures the thing most
#    ontologies neglect. The Trail has a label and a comment on every
#    class, so it scores 5; gist has a preferred label, a definition, and
#    often an example and a scope note, and scores higher still. The
#    coupling metrics, CBOOnto, RFCOnto, WMCOnto and NOMOnto, are the
#    software ones with the least ontological content: they add
#    superclasses and properties per class, and for both ontologies come
#    out low, which the thresholds read as good.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       metric      what                                    Trail         gist         note
#       AROnto      datatype properties per class           1.82 (5)      0.31 (2)     by declared domain
#       INROnto     object properties per class             1.47 (5)      0.12 (1)     gist keeps domains sparse, on purpose
#       RROnto      attributes among all properties         74% (4)       28% (2)
#       ANOnto      annotations per class                   2.0  (5)      3.6  (5)     the one that measures care
#       CROnto      individuals per class                   0    (1)      0.08 (1)     a schema has no individuals
#       RFCOnto     properties + superclasses per class     6.2  (3)      1.9  (5)
#       WMCOnto     properties and relationships per class  3.3  (5)      0.4  (5)
#
#       a metric counts what it can see; a principle it cannot see is counted as an absence
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - Richness metrics assign properties to classes by domain. Read
#      their small print before reading their scores.
#    - gist scores low on richness because it keeps domains sparse on
#      principle. The metric measured the principle, not the quality.
#    - ANOnto is the metric that tracks care. It is the one to put in a
#      gate.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser; the gist metrics on the MEASURED line come from scripts/metrics.py over gistCore.ttl
#  OPEN IN THE EDITOR
#            https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F10-quality%2Fo65-richness-and-coupling-what-the-metrics-count-and-what-a-style-choice-does-to-them.ttl
#  SOURCES   Duque-Ramos, Fernandez-Breis, Stevens and Aussenac-Gilles, OQuaRE: a SQuaRE-based approach for evaluating the quality of ontologies, JRPIT 43(2), 2011
#            http://www.cs.man.ac.uk/~stevensr/papers/OQuareProof.pdf
#            gist: a minimalist upper ontology
#            https://github.com/semanticarts/gist
#  MEASURED  parses, 6 triples; OQuaRE DIT 3 (4), ANOnto 3.63 (5)
# ==========================================================================

@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

<https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema>
    a                   owl:Ontology ;
    skos:prefLabel      "The Bookshop Trail schema"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote  "Richness and coupling metrics: MEASURED line for the Trail; gist in the diagram. Properties are assigned to classes by rdfs:domain, each member of a union counted; that is scripts/metrics.py's reading of a formula the paper leaves open."@en .
